RabbitMQ vs Kafka vs SQS — Practical Differences
The mental model

RabbitMQ = smart postman. It routes messages to the right consumer and forgets them once delivered.
Kafka = append-only log. It writes everything to disk; consumers read at their own pace and can re-read history.
SQS = AWS's managed queue. You don't manage anything; AWS handles scale, you just push and pull.

Where each one actually wins
RabbitMQ wins when: (50k messages/sec)

You need complex routing (this message goes to queue A and B, that one only to C)
Per-message acknowledgment matters (task queues, job processing)
You want priority queues, delayed messages, dead-letter queues out of the box
Low-to-medium throughput (~tens of thousands msg/sec)
Example: order processing pipeline, email/SMS dispatch, background jobs

Kafka wins when:

High throughput (millions msg/sec)
Multiple consumers need to read the same stream independently (analytics + audit + ML pipeline all reading order events)
You need to replay messages (reprocess last 7 days because a bug corrupted data)
Event sourcing, log aggregation, stream processing
Example: clickstream tracking, financial transaction logs, CDC from databases

SQS wins when: (120k messages/sec)

You're already on AWS and don't want to babysit infrastructure
Simple producer→queue→consumer pattern
Variable/spiky load (auto-scales)
You don't need ordering (Standard SQS) or you need light ordering (FIFO SQS, capped at 300 msg/sec)
Example: decoupling Lambda functions, simple work queues, async API processing

